Now I have a complete picture. Let me also double-check the SelfDies trigger vs. description check. Looking at the `collect_triggers` function in `triggers.rs`, for the `CreatureDied` event:

```rust
// 1. Self-dies trigger.
if registry.get(dead_card_id).is_some() {
    let desc = trigger_description(registry, dead_card_id, &crate::cards::TriggerKind::SelfDies, false);
    let trigger = PendingTrigger::SelfDies {
        dead_id,
        dead_card_id,
        controller: dead_controller,
        description: desc,
    };
    if dead_controller == active_player {
        ap_triggers.push(trigger);
    } else {
        nap_triggers.push(trigger);
    }
}
```

I see the issue now — the code unconditionally creates a SelfDies trigger for every registered card that dies, regardless of whether that card actually has a SelfDies ability defined. The `trigger_description` function returns an empty string when there's no matching trigger, but the trigger still gets pushed with that empty description. When it resolves, `behavior.on_dies` gets called anyway.

For cards without an actual `on_dies` implementation, the default is just a no-op, so it's harmless. Mausoleum Guard handles this fine.

